      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi @patrick@stwcx.xyz,\n\nThanks for calling this out — let me clarify where the \"01\" is coming from.\n\nYou’re right that if getHostNumStr() is viewed in isolation, the use of\nsubstr(len - 1) suggests that only a single character would be returned.\nThat’s also why this behavior is not obvious from that function alone.\n\nThe key detail is how the DBus object path is constructed.\n`POWER_DBUS_OBJECT_NAME` is defined as:\n```\n  \"/xyz/openbmc_project/Chassis/Buttons/Power0\"\n```\nIn button_factory, for multi-instance buttons, the object path is built as:\n```\n  getDbusObjectPath() + indexStr\n```\nAs a result, the actual DBus objects created are, for example:\n```\n  root@bmc:~# busctl tree xyz.openbmc_project.Chassis.Buttons\n  └─ /xyz/openbmc_project/Chassis/Buttons/Power01\n  └─ /xyz/openbmc_project/Chassis/Buttons/Power02\n  ...\n```\nGiven these zero-padded object names, the substring logic in getHostNumStr()\ncan return values like \"01\" rather than just \"1\". This is the concern I was\nraising, as it can lead to constructing an invalid chassis state path and\ncause the multi FIO power button to have no effect.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "e9f8de57654411e54bca341c166d4f4eaee923db",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
